Type Example Description <code>str</code> <code>"Hello"</code>, <code>'World'</code> Text/string <code>int</code> <code>10</code>, <code>-5</code>, <code>0</code> Whole numbers <code>float</code> <code>3.14</code>, <code>-0.5</code> Decimal numbers <code>bool</code> <code>True</code>, <code>False</code> Boolean values <code>list</code> <code>[1, 2, 3]</code> Ordered, mutable collection <code>tuple</code> <code>(1, 2, 3)</code> Ordered, immutable collection <code>set</code> <code>{1, 2, 3}</code> Unordered, unique items <code>dict</code> <code>{"key": "val"}</code> Key-value pairs <code>NoneType</code> <code>None</code> Represents nothing <pre><code># Check type of a value
print(type("Hello"))   # &lt;class 'str'&gt;
print(type(42))        # &lt;class 'int'&gt;
print(type(3.14))      # &lt;class 'float'&gt;
print(type(True))      # &lt;class 'bool'&gt;
</code></pre>
